WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.520
WordPress powers over 40% of the internet today.

00:00:04.520 --> 00:00:08.680
And while WordPress core is quite secure by now,

00:00:08.680 --> 00:00:12.120
if you've ever done anything on WordPress like I did,

00:00:12.120 --> 00:00:15.200
you know that you need WordPress plugins

00:00:15.200 --> 00:00:17.160
for even small things.

00:00:17.160 --> 00:00:20.520
And those plugins, they are not that secure,

00:00:20.520 --> 00:00:25.000
but the problem was not finding bugs for them in my past,

00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:27.959
the problem was monetizing them well.

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:30.680
But it has changed now, and in this video,

00:00:30.680 --> 00:00:33.439
I will show you both how to start

00:00:33.439 --> 00:00:37.080
auditing WordPress plugins and how to monetize them

00:00:37.080 --> 00:00:39.259
even if the plugin itself

00:00:39.259 --> 00:00:41.799
does not have a bug bounty program.

00:00:41.799 --> 00:00:42.639
Enjoy.

00:00:43.500 --> 00:00:45.959
This video is sponsored by WordFence,

00:00:45.959 --> 00:00:47.959
but I will tell you more about it later.

00:00:49.060 --> 00:00:51.520
So the first thing that you need for hacking WordPress

00:00:51.520 --> 00:00:53.520
is the WordPress instance.

00:00:53.520 --> 00:00:56.200
For this, I recommend you to use Docker

00:00:56.200 --> 00:00:58.720
and I actually use a Docker Compose file

00:00:58.720 --> 00:01:00.279
created by Justin from

00:01:00.279 --> 00:01:02.919
the Critical Thinking Bug Bounty Podcast.

00:01:02.919 --> 00:01:07.120
You just save this file locally, the Docker Compose YAML,

00:01:07.120 --> 00:01:09.879
and then you run Docker Compose app

00:01:09.879 --> 00:01:12.320
and it does everything for you.

00:01:12.320 --> 00:01:14.919
And your instance is ready in,

00:01:14.919 --> 00:01:16.199
it can take even a few minutes

00:01:16.199 --> 00:01:18.680
if you don't have the resources downloaded.

00:01:18.680 --> 00:01:21.839
And then you can access the local instance

00:01:21.839 --> 00:01:26.120
on localhost port 1337.

00:01:27.639 --> 00:01:32.639
You just have to go through the usual installation steps,

00:01:32.680 --> 00:01:35.160
doesn't matter things that you put in here

00:01:35.160 --> 00:01:37.040
because it's only for you anyway.

00:01:38.879 --> 00:01:43.040
And then you log in, you have your instance of WordPress.

00:01:43.040 --> 00:01:46.400
Then we are interested in WordPress plugins.

00:01:46.400 --> 00:01:51.360
So we'll just go to the plugins part

00:01:51.360 --> 00:01:56.360
and install, click add new plugin and install a plugin.

00:01:56.720 --> 00:02:01.720
I have a prepared one for the presentation.

00:02:05.360 --> 00:02:07.919
Is this one just click install?

00:02:07.919 --> 00:02:09.880
Then you possibly have to click activate

00:02:09.880 --> 00:02:11.720
or something like this.

00:02:11.720 --> 00:02:14.240
And then the plugin is installed.

00:02:14.240 --> 00:02:16.880
And always as with every website

00:02:16.880 --> 00:02:19.279
or every target that I test,

00:02:19.279 --> 00:02:21.880
the first thing is to just start using the thing.

00:02:21.880 --> 00:02:25.399
So in this case, I just need to use this shortcode

00:02:26.919 --> 00:02:30.039
on a page that will,

00:02:30.039 --> 00:02:34.399
so I can just see how normally the requests are sent.

00:02:34.399 --> 00:02:38.240
I can just paste it somewhere here, click update.

00:02:39.080 --> 00:02:42.320
And then when I see this page, I must be unauthenticated.

00:02:42.320 --> 00:02:47.320
So I will open it in the incognito window.

00:02:47.479 --> 00:02:52.440
Then I will see the feedback part

00:02:52.440 --> 00:02:56.839
and I can also submit any response.

00:02:58.679 --> 00:03:00.800
I can submit any response.

00:03:00.800 --> 00:03:05.800
And now I finally have some requests in Burb

00:03:06.199 --> 00:03:07.520
with my response.

00:03:07.520 --> 00:03:08.559
It's this one.

00:03:08.559 --> 00:03:10.039
