00:03:10.039 --> 00:03:11.080
Here's everything.

00:03:11.080 --> 00:03:15.440
And when doing the, when editing the source code,

00:03:15.440 --> 00:03:17.520
one of the first things I do,

00:03:17.520 --> 00:03:20.919
the big thing is I have a request here.

00:03:20.919 --> 00:03:24.600
And now I want to find the part of the source code

00:03:24.600 --> 00:03:28.039
that's responsible for this response.

00:03:28.039 --> 00:03:30.119
And usually the best,

00:03:30.119 --> 00:03:33.559
your best bet to do this in pretty much any technology

00:03:33.559 --> 00:03:37.800
is to just look by using the endpoint name.

00:03:37.800 --> 00:03:40.479
In this case, it's responses

00:03:40.479 --> 00:03:45.240
because we can also see this endpoint has the one number.

00:03:46.039 --> 00:03:49.479
It's probably the parameter, it's probably the form ID.

00:03:50.440 --> 00:03:55.279
And if you install the Docker the way I showed you,

00:03:55.279 --> 00:03:59.080
it creates the VPWP content directory

00:03:59.080 --> 00:04:00.800
and then the plugins are downloaded here.

00:04:00.800 --> 00:04:04.880
So you don't even have to download them manually

00:04:04.880 --> 00:04:07.080
to your computer.

00:04:07.080 --> 00:04:12.080
They are just synchronized in the folder outside Docker.

00:04:12.080 --> 00:04:13.600
And this is the plugin that I'm interested in.

00:04:13.600 --> 00:04:16.000
So I will mark find in folder.

00:04:16.000 --> 00:04:20.640
And now I'm only finding stuff inside this folder.

00:04:20.640 --> 00:04:24.720
I also want to limit this to PHP files.

00:04:24.720 --> 00:04:28.760
So I will do this and it gives us a few responses.

00:04:28.760 --> 00:04:32.200
Now, the part you are looking for now

00:04:32.200 --> 00:04:35.239
in WordPress is register rest route

00:04:35.239 --> 00:04:39.359
because this plugin created sort of a new endpoint

00:04:39.359 --> 00:04:40.279
in WordPress.

00:04:40.399 --> 00:04:42.119
And this is one of the ways

00:04:42.119 --> 00:04:45.440
that WordPress plugins use to do this.

00:04:45.440 --> 00:04:49.399
I will also show you another way to achieve this later.

00:04:50.239 --> 00:04:53.399
And the syntax of this is basically,

00:04:53.399 --> 00:04:54.799
here's the base path,

00:04:54.799 --> 00:04:56.320
which in this case is just a string,

00:04:56.320 --> 00:05:00.600
possibly it can be a constant or a variable or something.

00:05:00.600 --> 00:05:02.600
Then you have the endpoint name,

00:05:02.600 --> 00:05:06.559
which does match the endpoint I am looking for

00:05:06.559 --> 00:05:11.279
because I'm looking for this and it looks correctly.

00:05:11.279 --> 00:05:13.679
And then there are another arguments.

00:05:13.679 --> 00:05:16.519
Among others, there is an argument called methods.

00:05:16.519 --> 00:05:19.760
And we can see that this particular piece

00:05:19.760 --> 00:05:21.679
refers to a get method.

00:05:21.679 --> 00:05:25.000
But the request I am looking for is actually a post,

00:05:25.000 --> 00:05:29.200
which means this is not the part that I'm looking for.

00:05:29.200 --> 00:05:31.959
We can also see here a post,

00:05:31.959 --> 00:05:35.239
but this one is to responses slash trash.

00:05:35.279 --> 00:05:37.880
We can see responses restore also post,

00:05:37.880 --> 00:05:40.320
but none of these match my results.

00:05:40.320 --> 00:05:44.239
So I will go to another results here.

00:05:44.239 --> 00:05:47.679
And I can finally find another rest route

00:05:47.679 --> 00:05:50.160
that matches the route I am looking for

00:05:50.160 --> 00:05:52.440
and matches the method I am looking for.

00:05:52.440 --> 00:05:55.920
So I'm pretty convinced this is the one

00:05:55.920 --> 00:05:57.519
that I'm interested in.

00:05:57.519 --> 00:06:00.799
Now, the very important thing in the syntax

00:06:00.799 --> 00:06:05.200
of this register rest route arguments array

00:06:06.040 --> 00:06:07.440
is the permission callback,

00:06:07.440 --> 00:06:11.119
because it tells WordPress

00:06:11.119 --> 00:06:15.000
which users can perform a specific action.

00:06:15.000 --> 00:06:19.200
In this case, this means that it's just a function

00:06:19.200 --> 00:06:20.320
that always returns true.

00:06:20.320 --> 00:06:22.920
It's quite a strange PHP syntax.

00:06:22.920 --> 00:06:25.799
So it means this endpoint is unauthenticated

00:06:25.799 --> 00:06:27.040
and it makes a lot of sense,

00:06:27.040 --> 00:06:28.359
because as you remember,

00:06:28.359 --> 00:06:32.119
I just opened an incognito window to open this.

00:06:32.119 --> 00:06:35.359
So definitely it makes sense

00:06:35.359 --> 00:06:38.200
because this is a route available to anyone.

00:06:38.200 --> 00:06:40.239
And when you're looking for bugs,

00:06:40.239 --> 00:06:44.239
you should definitely focus on unauthenticated bugs

00:06:44.239 --> 00:06:47.040
because they just have the most impact.

00:06:47.040 --> 00:06:50.079
If we take a look at these endpoints,

00:06:51.279 --> 00:06:53.959
there's an actual function that does a permission check.

00:06:53.959 --> 00:06:57.640
Let's see how this looks like.

00:06:57.640 --> 00:07:00.519
We can find this function in the same file,

00:07:00.519 --> 00:07:03.040
which calls other function with this argument.

00:07:03.040 --> 00:07:05.279
So we have to look for this.

00:07:06.279 --> 00:07:08.880
It doesn't really tell us anything.

00:07:08.880 --> 00:07:12.399
There is a strange capabilities array.

00:07:12.399 --> 00:07:14.679
So this is what we are looking for now.

00:07:14.679 --> 00:07:16.399
And finally, after some time,

00:07:16.399 --> 00:07:21.399
we get to an array of what roles can perform what actions

00:07:22.040 --> 00:07:24.720
or what actions can be performed by what roles.

00:07:24.720 --> 00:07:26.399
This is not a standard way.

00:07:26.399 --> 00:07:29.279
There's no one specific way to do this,

00:07:29.279 --> 00:07:32.839
but generally by looking for the strings like I just did,

00:07:32.839 --> 00:07:37.720
I just searched for strings in this code.

00:07:37.720 --> 00:07:40.600
I can see that the view results capability,

00:07:40.600 --> 00:07:42.279
which is the one I'm interested in,

00:07:42.279 --> 00:07:45.200
can be performed by administrator or editor.

00:07:46.079 --> 00:07:49.119
Administrator by default in WordPress,

00:07:49.119 --> 00:07:51.160
administrator can install plugins

00:07:51.160 --> 00:07:54.079
so the administrator can execute code.

00:07:54.079 --> 00:07:57.679
So any vulnerability that requires

00:07:57.679 --> 00:08:01.239
the administrator capability is out of scope,

00:08:01.239 --> 00:08:03.040
at least the server side vulnerability

00:08:03.040 --> 00:08:06.399
where the attacker needs this account.

00:08:06.399 --> 00:08:08.519
Editor, it's a bit better

00:08:08.519 --> 00:08:12.000
because it's not the highest privilege level,

00:08:12.000 --> 00:08:13.359
but it's definitely better

00:08:13.359 --> 00:08:16.200
if you can exploit the bug unauthenticated

00:08:16.200 --> 00:08:18.679
or with the minimal permission level.

00:08:19.720 --> 00:08:24.720
So we'll come back to the code we're interested in.

00:08:25.079 --> 00:08:27.320
So these endpoints,

00:08:27.320 --> 00:08:31.040
they can be executed by administrators or editors.

00:08:31.040 --> 00:08:34.479
So I'm not looking for them at the moment

00:08:34.479 --> 00:08:38.520
because I want to find an unauthenticated vulnerability.

00:08:38.520 --> 00:08:40.960
So I come back to this route.

00:08:40.960 --> 00:08:42.919
So now we know what's permission callback,

00:08:42.919 --> 00:08:44.520
we know all the other parts,

00:08:44.520 --> 00:08:46.919
and then there's the callback argument,

00:08:46.919 --> 00:08:51.559
which is actually the function that processes the response.

00:08:51.559 --> 00:08:54.440
So I can look for the function with the name,

00:08:55.239 --> 00:08:57.559
very often in the same file.

00:08:57.559 --> 00:09:01.840
And now I have just found the, oops,

00:09:01.840 --> 00:09:06.840
I have just found the function that processed my response.

00:09:07.039 --> 00:09:08.840
We won't get into details of this

00:09:08.840 --> 00:09:10.799
because it's not that interesting actually,

00:09:10.799 --> 00:09:12.919
at least at the moment,

00:09:12.919 --> 00:09:17.919
but I have just found the code that processed this request.

00:09:18.119 --> 00:09:21.359
And this is for me always the first step

00:09:21.359 --> 00:09:23.880
when doing any kind of source code review.

00:09:24.719 --> 00:09:28.080
Before I get to another way that plugins can define routes,

00:09:28.080 --> 00:09:30.280
and I will also show you an actual bug

00:09:30.280 --> 00:09:34.239
in one of the plugins, why to even look for them?

00:09:34.239 --> 00:09:36.239
Because as I've told you in the intro,

00:09:36.239 --> 00:09:39.200
it used to be quite difficult to monetize them.

00:09:39.200 --> 00:09:43.400
Of course, if you have an unauthenticated severe bug,

00:09:43.400 --> 00:09:46.599
like server-side bug or an XSS,

00:09:46.599 --> 00:09:49.479
you can report the zero-day to many companies

00:09:49.479 --> 00:09:51.760
and possibly get paid for them.

00:09:51.760 --> 00:09:54.039
But there are a few problems with this.

00:09:54.640 --> 00:09:58.440
One problem is that often WordPress is out of scope

00:09:58.440 --> 00:10:01.719
and you will just not get paid for these bugs.

00:10:01.719 --> 00:10:04.679
The other problem is you need the bug

00:10:04.679 --> 00:10:08.039
to be either a server-side bug or an XSS

00:10:08.039 --> 00:10:12.000
because it cannot be anything with user interaction.

00:10:12.000 --> 00:10:14.359
Even if you have like an account takeover

00:10:14.359 --> 00:10:16.640
that only requires one click

00:10:16.640 --> 00:10:19.559
or requires the victim to open a website,

00:10:19.559 --> 00:10:21.400
that's too much for you to get paid.

00:10:21.400 --> 00:10:24.960
And the plugin developers

00:10:24.960 --> 00:10:28.000
usually don't host a bug bounty program.

00:10:28.000 --> 00:10:32.359
So these plugins, even though they power big part

00:10:32.359 --> 00:10:35.640
of the internet, were hard to monetize.

00:10:35.640 --> 00:10:38.400
But now there is WordFence bug bounty program,

00:10:38.400 --> 00:10:43.400
which covers every single plugin above 50,000 installs.

00:10:44.320 --> 00:10:47.440
And I think it's a really, really great opportunity.

00:10:47.440 --> 00:10:50.000
WordFence actually sponsors this video

00:10:50.039 --> 00:10:52.000
and I'm really happy to collaborate with them

00:10:52.000 --> 00:10:55.280
because I genuinely, I genuinely think

00:10:55.280 --> 00:10:57.679
it's a good opportunity to make money,

00:10:57.679 --> 00:11:00.000
to learn source code review

00:11:00.000 --> 00:11:03.479
because these plugins, they are not very secure.

00:11:03.479 --> 00:11:05.640
I looked at some of them

00:11:05.640 --> 00:11:09.840
and there are really, really a lot of holes in them.

00:11:09.840 --> 00:11:14.840
And above 50,000 installs, there are over 800 plugins

00:11:14.840 --> 00:11:19.039
which means millions of lines of PHP code

00:11:19.080 --> 00:11:21.000
that's potentially vulnerable.

00:11:21.000 --> 00:11:23.479
And if you're interested in taking part,

00:11:23.479 --> 00:11:28.479
go to bbre.dev slash WF, like WordFence.

00:11:28.559 --> 00:11:30.320
So let's come back to another way

00:11:30.320 --> 00:11:33.320
that a plugin might define a route.

00:11:33.320 --> 00:11:36.359
Let's install a plugin that you maybe know

00:11:36.359 --> 00:11:39.320
from the bounty blog, if you watched it carefully,

00:11:39.320 --> 00:11:43.799
the Monster Insights plugin for Google Analytics.

00:11:43.799 --> 00:11:47.760
It's one of the most popular plugins out there.

00:11:49.559 --> 00:11:53.000
And the way this plugin works is a bit different.

00:11:53.000 --> 00:11:54.799
When you interact with it,

00:11:54.799 --> 00:11:57.919
there are no requests to the endpoint

00:11:57.919 --> 00:12:00.200
like before with the rest route.

00:12:00.200 --> 00:12:05.200
There are only requests to admin.ajax.php

00:12:05.280 --> 00:12:07.719
and there's the action parameter

00:12:07.719 --> 00:12:09.960
which is actually crucial here

00:12:09.960 --> 00:12:14.960
because the other way I observed plugins defining routes

00:12:15.559 --> 00:12:20.280
was using the add action function.

00:12:20.280 --> 00:12:24.520
And the way it works is a bit similar to the rest route.

00:12:24.520 --> 00:12:26.599
First, there's the action name,

00:12:26.599 --> 00:12:30.679
which is actually the parameter that we can see here,

00:12:30.679 --> 00:12:35.679
the action, and then there's the callback function

00:12:36.440 --> 00:12:40.559
that will serve a particular request.

00:12:40.559 --> 00:12:43.479
And one difference between the register rest route

00:12:43.479 --> 00:12:46.200
and this that you should immediately see

00:12:46.200 --> 00:12:49.440
that first there is no method here,

00:12:49.440 --> 00:12:51.919
but it's not as big of a problem,

00:12:51.919 --> 00:12:56.719
but there's also no defined way for the permission check

00:12:56.719 --> 00:12:58.200
in the register rest route.

00:12:58.200 --> 00:13:00.880
We saw the particular argument,

00:13:00.880 --> 00:13:03.640
which was a function to verify permissions.

00:13:03.640 --> 00:13:06.239
In this case, there's nothing like this.

00:13:06.239 --> 00:13:09.280
So in every single controller,

00:13:09.280 --> 00:13:11.559
the code should verify

00:13:11.599 --> 00:13:13.919
if the current user has capabilities

00:13:13.919 --> 00:13:16.960
or has permissions to make a particular action.

00:13:16.960 --> 00:13:20.080
If you are in security for a longer time,

00:13:20.080 --> 00:13:23.640
you know that if developers have to do something

00:13:23.640 --> 00:13:28.599
over and over and over again like this in the if statement,

00:13:28.599 --> 00:13:30.840
eventually they will forget about it.

00:13:30.840 --> 00:13:34.159
So there are definitely loads

00:13:34.159 --> 00:13:37.919
of access control bugs with this.

00:13:37.919 --> 00:13:40.919
And apart from this, there's PHP code,

00:13:41.359 --> 00:13:44.880
which processes the response.

00:13:44.880 --> 00:13:47.320
And unfortunately, I can't tell you

00:13:47.320 --> 00:13:49.239
how to start hacking WordPress plugins.

00:13:49.239 --> 00:13:51.119
I can tell you how to define routes,

00:13:51.119 --> 00:13:53.200
but eventually what you need

00:13:53.200 --> 00:13:55.479
is you need to be able to read this code,

00:13:55.479 --> 00:13:57.960
analyze it and find bugs.

00:13:57.960 --> 00:14:01.440
So let's come back to the user feedback plugin

00:14:01.440 --> 00:14:06.440
and let's actually find a real vulnerability.

00:14:07.440 --> 00:14:11.960
One of the ways that I like to search the code

00:14:11.960 --> 00:14:16.760
for vulnerabilities is to use a regular expression like this.

00:14:16.760 --> 00:14:19.599
It means there's an apostrophe,

00:14:19.599 --> 00:14:23.039
which means I am looking for something inside the string.

00:14:23.039 --> 00:14:25.080
If the target would be using double quotes,

00:14:25.080 --> 00:14:26.599
I would also have double quotes here.

00:14:26.599 --> 00:14:28.239
If it would be JavaScript,

00:14:28.239 --> 00:14:30.960
I will also probably have backticks here.

00:14:30.960 --> 00:14:35.359
Then I'm looking for opening the HTML tag.

00:14:35.400 --> 00:14:36.840
Then for anything,

00:14:36.840 --> 00:14:41.080
and then I'm looking for the way to insert user data.

00:14:41.080 --> 00:14:44.760
So essentially I'm looking for string concatenation

00:14:44.760 --> 00:14:46.400
that uses HTML tags,

00:14:46.400 --> 00:14:49.760
because then I know that if HTML tags

00:14:49.760 --> 00:14:52.119
will be inside concatenated strings,

00:14:52.119 --> 00:14:55.719
potentially I can look for XSS.

00:14:55.719 --> 00:14:59.159
And in PHP, they use %s

00:14:59.159 --> 00:15:02.520
to define the variable inside the string.

00:15:02.520 --> 00:15:04.559
So that's why I just have the percentage

00:15:04.599 --> 00:15:07.679
because sometimes it's not S, it's something else.

00:15:07.679 --> 00:15:10.320
But in general, it does work.

00:15:10.320 --> 00:15:12.119
There are a few results.

00:15:12.119 --> 00:15:15.599
And I want to look at this one in particular,

00:15:15.599 --> 00:15:17.719
because I know it leads somewhere.

00:15:18.719 --> 00:15:23.719
It has the A element with the href attribute

00:15:25.080 --> 00:15:27.159
